# Nothing in this repository is generated, and nothing in it is secret.
#
# These entries exist to catch the two ways that could accidentally change: a
# credential file created in the wrong place, or an editor/tool dropping state
# into a directory that gets committed.

# Never commit a real credential file, wherever someone puts one.
secrets/
*.secret
*.key
!home/.local/bin/herdr-agy.sh

# Installer output and backups, in case someone runs the scripts from the repo.
*.bak-yellowstone-*
*.tmp

# A generated inventory of THIS machine's skills and themes. Useful locally,
# never ship it: it lists skills for private projects and names absolute paths.
# Regenerate with the description in docs/testing.md.
agent-skills.md

# Editor and OS noise
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
.idea/
.vscode/

# Node, if anyone runs a tool here
node_modules/
npm-debug.log*

# research/ is sole-copy working notes, not a publication channel. Only deliberately published
# findings are tracked; everything else stays on disk (a verified safety copy lives outside the
# publication tree). Rules make the accident unavailable rather than relying on remembering not to
# 'git add -A' a 21 MB tree.
#
# It MUST be 'research/*' and not 'research/': git never descends into an ignored DIRECTORY, so with
# 'research/' every negation below is inert (measured in a throwaway repo: 'research/' matched even
# the file named by a following '!research/keep.md'). Matching the contents instead lets a negation
# through, which is what makes publishing a finding a one-line change.
#
# A negated file inside a SUBDIRECTORY needs its directory re-included first ('!research/<dir>/'
# before '!research/<dir>/<file>'); a bare nested negation stays ignored.
research/*
!research/si-into-yellowstone-merge-plan.md
!research/tui-render-spike-findings.md
#
# These rules change what `git status` ANSWERS, so it is no longer the instrument for 'is there
# untracked research output?' — it returns a confident zero for that question whether the tree is
# empty or merely ignored. The instruments that do answer it:
#   enumerate:  find research/ -type f
#   integrity:  (cd ~/.local/state/yellowstone/research-sole-copies-<stamp> && sha256sum -c MANIFEST.sha256)
# The second answers 'is the copy intact', NOT 'is every live note copied': it is a snapshot, so a note
# added afterwards is missing from both the copy and its manifest. That second question needs a set
# comparison of manifest paths against `find` output (comm on the sorted lists), and it is the one whose
# absence is silent. Snapshot last extended 2026-11-27.

# Python bytecode from the overlord tools, created by merely running them
__pycache__/
